# CODEOWNERS -- who GitHub requests for review when a pull request touches a
# path. Ownership here is a request, not a permission. It grants no access and
# blocks no merge on its own; a branch protection rule or ruleset with "Require
# review from Code Owners" is what makes it binding, and that lives in the
# repository settings rather than in this file.
#
# Read from .github/, then the repository root, then docs/ -- in that order,
# first one found wins, and nowhere else. Two copies is a silent trap: the one
# in .github/ is used and the other is never read. This file ships at the root.
# Move it if you want it elsewhere; do not copy it.
#
# Rules are read per branch, from the BASE branch of a pull request. Fixing
# this file on a feature branch does not change that branch's own reviewers.
#
# Everything here fails silently:
#
#   - An invalid line is skipped. The rest of the file still applies, so a typo
#     removes one rule and nothing says so.
#   - A user or team without write access is never assigned. Naming someone who
#     cannot review looks exactly like naming nobody. A team must additionally
#     be visible and hold write access in its own right, even when every member
#     already has it.
#   - Over 3 MB the entire file is ignored.
#   - Draft pull requests request no one; owners are notified when the pull
#     request is marked ready for review.
#
# Two of those are checkable, and nothing checks them unless asked: browse to
# this file on GitHub, where rejected lines are highlighted, or run
#
#     gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/codeowners/errors
#
# Patterns use gitignore syntax minus three of its features: `!` negation,
# `[ ]` character ranges, and `\#` to escape a leading `#`. GitHub documents
# all three as not working and does not say what happens instead -- a `!` line
# has been reported both highlighted as invalid and accepted as valid -- so the
# two checks above catch one only sometimes. Do not reach for them. Paths are
# case sensitive whatever the local filesystem does.
#
# THE LAST MATCHING PATTERN WINS -- not the most specific one. That is the
# reverse of most path-matching intuition and the first mistake to look for.
# Specific rules go BELOW the catch-all, never above it.
#
# Several owners for one pattern go on ONE line. Split across two lines, the
# second replaces the first instead of adding to it. Any one owner on a line
# satisfies a required code-owner review, so a line names a pool, not a quorum.
#
# A pattern with no owner after it UNSETS ownership for that path, which is how
# a subdirectory is exempted from a broader rule.

# The default owner for everything. Replace the placeholder below with a GitHub
# username (@octocat) or a team (@org/team-name), keeping the @. An email
# address registered to an account with write access also resolves. Replacing
# it should leave no occurrence of the placeholder anywhere in this file, which
# is what makes grepping for it a straight answer to whether this was done.
#
# That line ships active and wrong rather than commented out, deliberately. It
# is the one placeholder in these addons that reports itself: GitHub validates
# CODEOWNERS, so an unfilled line is highlighted on the file's page and listed
# by the API call above, while a commented-out file is indistinguishable from a
# finished one. Until it is filled the file assigns no owners, which is the
# truth about it.
* @REPLACE-CODE-OWNER

# Ownership per path, for when more than one person owns something. Uncomment
# and adjust -- these mirror the layout in the root CLAUDE.md. Every one of
# them must stay below the catch-all above to take effect.
#
# /apps/      @org/team-name
# /libs/      @org/team-name
# /scripts/   @org/team-name
# /docs/adr/  @org/team-name
#
# A leading slash anchors to the repository root: `/docs/` is the top-level
# directory and everything under it, while `docs/` with no leading slash is any
# directory named docs at any depth. A slash in the MIDDLE of a pattern anchors
# it as well, so `docs/*` is the files sitting directly in the top-level docs --
# neither its subdirectories nor a docs directory nested somewhere else.

# The rules governing who may change the rules. This is GitHub's own
# recommendation once more than one account has write access: without it,
# anyone can edit this file, or the workflows beside it, in the same pull
# request that depends on the edit. Redundant while one owner owns everything
# above.
#
# /.github/    @org/team-name
# /CODEOWNERS  @org/team-name

# One caution for a solo maintainer, because it is otherwise discovered at the
# worst moment: GitHub does not let the author of a pull request approve it.
# `* @you` plus a rule requiring code-owner review means every pull request you
# open needs an approval that only you can give and that you are not allowed to
# give. The file is still worth having -- it records ownership and is already
# correct when a second maintainer arrives -- but leave that requirement off
# until someone else can satisfy it. Delete this paragraph once decided.
